  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "What happens to each email",
      "items": [
        "Allowed sender with rsbulletin.html attached: saved to the website bucket, CloudFront cache cleared, admin told it worked",
        "Allowed sender, no rsbulletin.html: automatic reply asking them to resend",
        "Sender not on the allowlist: the message is forwarded to the admin and nothing is published",
        "Allowed sender's address, but SES can't confirm they sent it (SPF and DMARC both fail) or it fails the spam or virus scan: nothing is published and the admin gets a \"Bulletin email rejected\" notice",
        "Any other address at the domain: the catch-all rule runs the Lambda without storing the email, and the admin gets a wrong-recipient alert"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Deploying",
      "items": [
        "One CloudFormation stack, email-pdf-ingest in us-east-1, creates every AWS resource shown",
        "Two phases: NameserversReady=false first, then true once the domain points at Route 53, which adds the certificate, aliases and A records",
        "The SES rule set has to be activated by hand after the first deploy",
        "deploy.sh reads the current settings back from the Lambda, redeploys the stack, then syncs s3-files/ into the website bucket"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Worth knowing",
      "items": [
        "The editor downloads ReliefSocietyBulletin_<date>.html, but the Lambda only accepts an attachment named rsbulletin.html, so the file must be renamed first",
        "Because deploy.sh syncs s3-files/, the editor page is also served by CloudFront to anyone with its URL",
        "CloudFront sends missing pages to /error.html, a page-not-found page in s3-files/ styled like the bulletin"
      ]
    }
  ]
